  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1210 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nWednesday night through Monday:\n\nSynopsis: The frontal boundary encroaching during the end of the \nshort term will linger regionally through the second half of the \nwork week, ebbing and flowing between stationary to at-times warm \nfrontal in its behavior. Above normal temperatures & muggy \nconditions will thus continue into at least Friday, with afternoon \nheat indices in the low to mid 90s remaining commonplace. Similar to \nthe short term period, heat indices in the upper 90s to near 100 \nwill be possible in locations experiencing prolonged periods of \nafternoon sunshine. The lingering surface front & entrenched muggy \nair mass will keep conditions active Wednesday night into at least \nthe first half of the weekend, with periods of off & on \nthunderstorms expected to continue. Will need to continue to watch \nfor a few severe storms, particularly from Wednesday night through \nFriday evening.  \n\nWednesday Evening through Saturday: Muggy and active weather \nconditions continue as a surface front lingers over the western \nGreat Lakes. Anticipating similar heat index trends through each \nafternoon period, with the more localized nature of any upper 90s to \nnear 100 degree readings likely precluding the need for any heat \nheadlines. Will nevertheless be monitoring trends closely, as any \nsmall upward trends would call for reconsideration. Will also be \nmonitoring for daily storm development. Expecting similar storm \ndevelopment behavior as the short term through Friday, with a \nseasonably strong low level jet supporting bouts of nocturnal storms \n& daytime development being contingent on the presence & favorable \nplacement of surface boundaries. A combination of sufficient deep \nlayer shear and very high instability will make any storms ones to \nwatch for severe weather hazards during the Wednesday evening - \nFriday time frame. Anticipating more of a transition to disorganized \ndaytime convection moving into Saturday as both the low level jet & \ndeep layer shear weaken considerably.\n\nQuigley",
